I’ll inspect the diffraction data and metadata first, then identify candidate phases, build/refine the multiphase model, and verify that every reported CIF, fraction, profile, and statistic comes from the saved final GSAS-II project.

The chemistry points toward copper tungstates, but the phase count is explicitly unknown, so I’ll treat CuO, WO₃, CuWO₄, and plausible Cu–W–O polymorphs/residuals as competing hypotheses. I’m now extracting peak positions and checking the installed indexing/database/GSAS-II interfaces before selecting structures.

The peak fingerprint strongly supports triclinic wolframite-type CuWO₄: the low-angle lines near 15.61° and 19.32° and the dense 23–39° multiplet are characteristic. The database’s CuWO₄ entry is a pressure-contracted cell, however, so I’m using it only as an atomic-coordinate model and will refine the cell against this ambient-pattern dataset. I’ll test residual CuO/WO₃ and other Cu–W–O candidates by whether they explain unmatched peaks and materially improve the fit.

The peak offsets are internally consistent with an ambient CuWO₄ cell plus an approximately +0.30° instrument/sample zero offset; this is much stronger evidence than simply matching a few peaks. I’ve initialized a full-pattern Cu Kα₁/Kα₂ Rietveld model with counting-statistical weights and will now refine it in stable blocks, then inspect the difference curve for any coherent unmodeled phase.

The one-phase model already reproduces all major and minor peak positions; the residual maxima sit on CuWO₄ reflections rather than forming a second-phase fingerprint. I’m now testing only defensible refinements—atomic coordinates, polarization, preferred orientation, and broadening terms—one at a time, while removing a redundant scale parameter that the importer enabled by default.

A closer residual check does reveal weak, chemically plausible precursor signatures: positive residuals near the expected zero-shifted CuO lines (~35.8°, 39.0°, 49.0°) and monoclinic WO₃ lines (~23.4–24.7° and ~34°). I’m therefore testing CuO and room-temperature monoclinic WO₃ explicitly as minor phases; they will be retained only if their scales are positive, their full reflection sets are supported, and the improvement survives refinement.

Monoclinic WO₃ is supported across its full line set and refines to about 2.16 wt%; it lowers Rwp from 7.43% to 6.75%. CuO is driven to the zero-scale boundary, while Cu₃WO₆ gives only a marginal ~0.16 wt% signal that is not robust enough to claim. I’m doing a final model-selection pass on profile asymmetry, texture, and anisotropic broadening, then I’ll lock the simplest physically credible model and export directly from it.

The optional model tests show that a small axial-divergence asymmetry term is supported (Rwp 6.54% versus 6.75%), while preferred orientation is essentially unity and bounded ADPs/generalized strain become over-parameterized or nonphysical. I’ll keep the asymmetry correction, omit texture and anisotropic broadening, set the ineffective negative Lorentzian term to zero, and settle the refinement before the final impurity check.

The apparent final-profile inconsistency was a GSAS-II bookkeeping issue: after changing cells, the stored reflection list/profile lagged the refined parameters. A zero-cycle recalculation regenerates the reflection lists and gives a consistent Rwp of 6.54%. I’ll preserve the covariance matrix from the converged refinement, then perform that calculation-only pass in the submitted project so the CIFs, fractions, and fit.csv all correspond exactly.

The two-phase refinement is converged. I’m now packaging the clean GSAS-II project and exports, then I’ll cross-check that the CIFs, fractions, profile, and reported statistics all reproduce the final project.

The deliverables are now generated: CuWO₄ refines to 97.80 wt% and monoclinic WO₃ to 2.20 wt%, with \(R_\mathrm{wp}=6.53\%\). I’m checking the exported structures and project/profile consistency before writing the uncertainty note.

Validation passed: both CIFs parse with the intended compositions and space groups, the fractions sum exactly to one, and every exported profile value matches the stored `final.gpx` calculation. The minor-phase coordinate limitation and crystalline-only normalization are documented in the report.

Completed and validated the final two-phase model:

- CuWO₄ (P -1): 97.80(6) wt%
- WO₃ (P 2₁/c): 2.20(6) wt%
- \(R_\mathrm{wp}=6.525\%\), \(R_\mathrm{p}=4.734\%\), GOF = 2.689

Fractions are normalized over crystalline phases; amorphous content was not determined. WO₃ atomic coordinates could not be independently refined reliably at its low abundance.

